Suppose the cylinder is actually a beer can, so that it has approximate dimensions of r = 1 in and h = 5 in. 
Then 

		dV = π(10Δr + Δh). 

This statement shows that, for these particular values of r and h, the volume is approxmately 10 times more sensitive to change in radius than changes in height. 
That is, if the radius is changed by and amount e, then the height must be changed by roughly 10e to keep the volume constant (i.e., to make ΔV zero). 
We use the word "approximate" because our analysis arises from considering the differetial change dV rather than the actual incremental change ΔV. 

This beer can example has real application to product marketing strategies. 
Because the volume is so much more sensitive to changes in radius than height, it is possible to make a can appear to be larger than standard by decreasing its radius slightly (little enough so as to be hardly noticeable) and increasing the height so no change in volume results. 
